  "executive_summary": "This study investigates whether the length of chronic total occlusion (CTO) lesions impacts six-month angiographic and two-year clinical outcomes following successful percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) with drug-eluting stents (DES). A total of 235 consecutive patients were enrolled and divided into long CTO (≥30mm) and short CTO (<30mm) groups. Baseline characteristics were similar except for higher prior PCI in the long group and more bifurcation lesions in the short group. In-hospital complications were comparable, though intimal dissection was more frequent in the long CTO group. At six months, angiographic outcomes, including in-stent restenosis and binary restenosis, were statistically similar between groups, although a numerical trend toward higher restenosis was observed in the long group. Over two years, major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE), including death and myocardial infarction, did not differ significantly. However, the incidence of repeat PCI, predominantly target vessel revascularization (TVR), was significantly higher in the long CTO group (18.8% vs. 7.8%). Multivariate analysis identified long CTO as an independent predictor for repeat PCI (OR 4.26). The authors conclude that while long CTO lesions are associated with higher rates of repeat revascularization, they do not translate into worse hard endpoints like mortality or MI, suggesting similar overall safety and efficacy profiles in the DES era.",
